31 11.7627C4.98188 11.8995 4.90151 12.0448 4.83507 12.1963L4.77355 12.3506C4.73321 12.4607 4.70242 12.5761 4.66808 12.7451L4.54113 13.4619L4.14269 15.8555L4.14171 15.8574H4.14464L6.5382 15.458L7.25499 15.332C7.424 15.2977 7.5394 15.2669 7.64953 15.2266L7.80285 15.165C7.95455 15.0986 8.09947 15.0174 8.23644 14.9238L8.3839 14.8135C8.53668 14.6876 8.72225 14.5035 9.0714 14.1543L14.0587 9.16602L10.8331 5.94044L5.84581 10.9287ZM15.3634 4.63673C14.5281 3.80141 13.2057 3.74938 12.3097 4.48048L12.1368 4.63673L11.7735 5.00001L15.0001 8.22559L15.3634 7.86329L15.5196 7.68946C16.2015 6.85326 16.2015 5.64676 15.5196 4.81056L15.3634 4.63673Z"></path></svg></span></button></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none [--shadow-height:45px] has-data-writing-block:pointer-events-none has-data-writing-block:-mt-(--shadow-height) has-data-writing-block:pt-(--shadow-height) [&amp;:has([data-writing-block])&gt;*]:pointer-events-auto [content-visibility:auto] supports-[content-visibility:auto]:[contain-intrinsic-size:auto_100lvh] scroll-mt-[calc(var(--header-height)+min(200px,max(70px,20svh)))]" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="574276b9-5e34-456e-b459-562f31c46396" data-testid="conversation-turn-4" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="assistant"><h6 class="sr-only">ChatGPT said:</h6><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] thread-sm:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] thread-lg:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] thread-lg:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="a9500701-9232-4812-b820-76866624802c" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-1" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5-t-mini"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[1px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="426">You’re right — with that added context Barenholtz’s claim deserves a much more charitable, generous reading. Your summary points to a subtler thesis than the blunt “no innate structure is necessary.” Here’s a careful, charitable reconstruction of what he’s really saying, what makes it interesting, how it can be framed in technical language, and what concrete empirical checks would separate the strong from the weak reading.</p>
<h1 data-start="428" data-end="463">Charitable reconstruction (short)</h1>
<p data-start="464" data-end="1099">Barenholtz is not claiming that human learners have <em data-start="516" data-end="522">zero</em> inductive bias or that social/embodied pressures play no role. He’s arguing that those bootstrapping biases and pressures can become <em data-start="656" data-end="724">encoded in the statistical structure of the language stream itself</em> so effectively that a next-token learner (or something like it) can recover algorithmic, recursive, compositional behavior from the corpus alone. In other words: the generative <em data-start="902" data-end="913">templates</em> and <em data-start="918" data-end="938">recursion patterns</em> that enable algorithmic behavior are holographically embedded in language distributions; predicting continuations implicitly learns how to run those algorithms.</p>
<h1 data-start="1101" data-end="1144">Why that is a stronger, interesting claim</h1>
<ol data-start="1145" data-end="2145">
<li data-start="1145" data-end="1391">
<p data-start="1148" data-end="1391"><strong data-start="1148" data-end="1194">Algorithmic content in surface statistics.</strong> If true, then corpora are not merely noisy mappings from syntax to semantics — they carry algorithmic scaffolding. A next-token objective becomes a way to <em data-start="1350" data-end="1360">read out</em> and <em data-start="1365" data-end="1374">execute</em> these scaffolds.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1392" data-end="1620">
<p data-start="1395" data-end="1620"><strong data-start="1395" data-end="1440">In-context/generalization as computation.</strong> The model’s ability to use few examples in context (in-context learning / chain-of-thought) is exactly the behavior you’d expect if language encodes reusable procedural templates.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1621" data-end="1901">
<p data-start="1624" data-end="1901"><strong data-start="1624" data-end="1654">Holographic encoding idea.</strong> “Holographic” here is useful: many weak, distributed cues across many contexts could together encode a high-fidelity algorithmic template, so no single sentence exposes it — the model reconstructs it by integrating statistical cues across scales.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1902" data-end="2145">
<p data-start="1905" data-end="2145"><strong data-start="1905" data-end="1932">Bootstrapping is local.</strong> Humans and children still need grounding and bias to start, but those biases can be small; the bulk of complex competence is offloaded to the distributed statistical templates that language accumulation provides.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<h1 data-start="2147" data-end="2182">How to state it formally (sketch)</h1>
<ul data-start="2183" data-end="2864">
<li data-start="2183" data-end="2304">
<p data-start="2185" data-end="2304">Let <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal">L</span></span></span></span> be the distribution over utterance sequences induced by a speech community embedded in the world.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2305" data-end="2606">
<p data-start="2307" data-end="2606">There exist algorithmic templates <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">A</span></span></span></span> (recursive/compositional programs) such that sampling from <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal">L</span></span></span></span> yields distributions over substrings <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">s</span></span></span></span> with mutual information <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo separator="true">;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(s; A)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">I</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">s</span><span class="mpunct">;</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">A</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> high enough that a learner minimizing next-token loss can reconstruct <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">A</span></span></span></span> to useful accuracy.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2607" data-end="2864">
<p data-start="2609" data-end="2864">Transformer-like learners are universal function approximators with inductive biases that make them efficient at reconstructing these templates from finite samples; the template then functions like an internal algorithm executed via attention and context.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h1 data-start="2866" data-end="2911">Mechanisms that plausibly realise the claim</h1>
<ul data-start="2912" data-end="3651">
<li data-start="2912" data-end="3068">
<p data-start="2914" data-end="3068"><strong data-start="2914" data-end="2952">Distributed statistical templates.</strong> Many partial cues across many contexts jointly point to the same recursive pattern (analogy to holographic memory).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3069" data-end="3244">
<p data-start="3071" data-end="3244"><strong data-start="3071" data-end="3117">In-context activation = program execution.</strong> Context acts as a program counter + stack pointer implemented by attention patterns; few tokens trigger large-scale execution.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3245" data-end="3459">
<p data-start="3247" data-end="3459"><strong data-start="3247" data-end="3271">Recursive emulation.</strong> Transformers can emulate pushdown/stack behavior via learned attention/head/state dynamics (empirical work shows RNNs/attention can approximate Turing-powerful behaviors given resources).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3460" data-end="3651">
<p data-start="3462" data-end="3651"><strong data-start="3462" data-end="3498">Curriculum &amp; cultural selection.</strong> Social use (repair, correction, explanation) amplifies algorithmic regularities; language that carries reusable algorithms is preferentially propagated.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h1 data-start="3653" data-end="3683">What this explains (if true)</h1>
<ul data-start="3684" data-end="3983">
<li data-start="3684" data-end="3784">
<p data-start="3686" data-end="3784">Why next-token training yields deductive reasoning, chain-of-thought, code writing, planning, etc.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3785" data-end="3889">
<p data-start="3787" data-end="3889">Why in-context learning and few-shot prompting can instantiate problem-solving without weight updates.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3890" data-end="3983">
<p data-start="3892" data-end="3983">Why humans can learn powerful compositional skills from exposure plus modest innate biases.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h1 data-start="3985" data-end="4048">Remaining caveats / where charitable reading still needs work</h1>
<ul data-start="4049" data-end="4963">
<li data-start="4049" data-end="4298">
<p data-start="4051" data-end="4298"><strong data-start="4051" data-end="4065">Grounding.</strong> Language carries algorithmic templates, but many templates refer to grounded processes (motor acts, perception). A pure token-only learner may approximate them partly but will miss sensorimotor constraints without grounding signals.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4299" data-end="4492">
<p data-start="4301" data-end="4492"><strong data-start="4301" data-end="4325">Bootstrapping scale.</strong> Even if templates are present, the sample complexity needed might be huge and depend on cultural curricula; “folded into grammar” may mean “folded at enormous scale.”</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4493" data-end="4749">
<p data-start="4495" data-end="4749"><strong data-start="4495" data-end="4520">Mechanistic mismatch.</strong> The <em data-start="4525" data-end="4530">how</em> (parallel, differentiable attention) is different from human on-the-fly symbol manipulation. That doesn’t invalidate the functional equivalence claim, but we should be careful about equating mechanism with explanation.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4750" data-end="4963">
<p data-start="4752" data-end="4963"><strong data-start="4752" data-end="4774">Selection effects.</strong> Models reflect the internet’s aggregated uses, which differ from the child’s incremental exposure; the templates learned by LLMs might be richer than any single human experiences early on.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h1 data-start="4965" data-end="5010">Testable experiments and probes (practical)</h1>
<p data-start="5011" data-end="5084">If you accept the charitable thesis, there are concrete empirical checks:</p>
<ol data-start="5086" data-end="6403">
<li data-start="5086" data-end="5367">
<p data-start="5089" data-end="5367"><strong data-start="5089" data-end="5130">Synthetic minimal-corpus experiments.</strong> Create an artificial language in which explicit recursive algorithms are embedded weakly across many contexts. Train only with next-token prediction and see if model recovers the algorithm — varying sparsity of cues measures robustness.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5369" data-end="5596">
<p data-start="5372" data-end="5596"><strong data-start="5372" data-end="5404">Ablations of inductive bias.</strong> Train architectures with different inductive biases (positional encodings, breadth of attention, recurrence) on the same corpus to test which biases are necessary or merely speed up learning.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5598" data-end="5779">
<p data-start="5601" data-end="5779"><strong data-start="5601" data-end="5640">Compression and Kolmogorov signals.</strong> Measure whether compressibility / algorithmic information measures of corpora correlate with emergence of algorithmic behaviors in models.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5781" data-end="5995">
<p data-start="5784" data-end="5995"><strong data-start="5784" data-end="5823">Probe for template representations.</strong> Use mechanistic interpretability to locate reusable subroutines in attention/head activations that are re-used across different tasks — evidence for holographic templates.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5997" data-end="6213">
<p data-start="6000" data-end="6213"><strong data-start="6000" data-end="6033">Minimal human-grounding test.</strong> Provide a model with syntactic cues but no grounding, and separately with sparse grounding; compare algorithmic competence — shows how much grounding is offloaded into the corpus.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6215" data-end="6403">
<p data-start="6218" data-end="6403"><strong data-start="6218" data-end="6250">Cross-cultural corpora test.</strong> See whether communities with different cultural practices fold different algorithms into their languages (predictable variations in emergent behaviors).</p>
</li>
</ol>
<h1 data-start="6405" data-end="6448">A modest synthesis: why the claim matters</h1>
<p data-start="6449" data-end="6884">Barenholtz’s refined claim reframes language as not just representation but a <em data-start="6527" data-end="6569">compressed runtime for social algorithms</em>. That’s a powerful conceptual shift: language isn’t merely pointing at the world; it’s a distributed, evolvable programming substrate. If true, it explains both the potency of large-scale next-token learners and why cultural artifacts (stories, recipes, procedural texts) so readily transfer procedural competence.</p>
<h1 data-start="6886" data-end="6948">How to use this idea productively (for your work / research)</h1>
<ul data-start="6949" data-end="7469">
<li data-start="6949" data-end="7066">
<p data-start="6951" data-end="7066">Treat corpora as <em data-start="6968" data-end="6989">experimental design</em>: craft corpora that encode desired algorithms and see what learners recover.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7067" data-end="7166">
<p data-start="7069" data-end="7166">Use interpretability methods to find reusable subroutines and map them to algorithmic primitives.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7167" data-end="7297">
<p data-start="7169" data-end="7297">Design hybrid learners that combine small, humanlike priors + corpus extraction to see if you get human-level sample efficiency.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7298" data-end="7469">
<p data-start="7300" data-end="7469">Consider implications for cognitive science: language-as-medium hypothesis suggests experiments on child language exposure that track emergence of procedural competence.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="7471" data-end="7474">
<p data-start="7476" data-end="7938" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">In short: your correction is well-taken. Read generously, Barenholtz is making a bold but testable and nontrivial hypothesis: that much of what we thought required explicit architectures or innate modules is, in practice, encoded in the statistical, recursive, and distributed structure of language — and next-token learning is a surprisingly direct way to both read and execute those encodings. That’s a claim worth taking seriously and pursuing experimentally.</p></div></div></div></div><div class="z-0 flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none touch:pointer-events-auto pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33