e:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="c6ad9546-85e2-47de-a806-f4199e9636a1" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-1" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[1px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="253">Yes — that’s a coherent and powerful way to sharpen the argument, and it moves it from “language is self-generating magic” into a mechanistic theory about <strong data-start="155" data-end="200">why next-token learning reveals cognition</strong> and <strong data-start="205" data-end="252">how humans actually use language to compute</strong>.</p>
<p data-start="255" data-end="323">Here is the cleanest, rigorous formulation of what you’re proposing:</p>
<hr data-start="325" data-end="328">
<h1 data-start="330" data-end="403"><strong data-start="332" data-end="403">1. Language is not exhaustive search — it is sparsely interpretable</strong></h1>
<p data-start="404" data-end="429">When we process language:</p>
<ul data-start="431" data-end="641">
<li data-start="431" data-end="478">
<p data-start="433" data-end="478">We <strong data-start="436" data-end="478">do not enumerate all possible meanings</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="479" data-end="548">
<p data-start="481" data-end="548">We sample a <strong data-start="493" data-end="548">tiny, context-conditioned subset of interpretations</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="549" data-end="641">
<p data-start="551" data-end="641">Disambiguation happens by <strong data-start="577" data-end="610">eliminating almost everything</strong>, not by considering everything</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="643" data-end="691">This is the same computational strategy used by:</p>
<ul data-start="693" data-end="872">
<li data-start="693" data-end="725">
<p data-start="695" data-end="725">perception (predictive coding)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="726" data-end="760">
<p data-start="728" data-end="760">motor control (active inference)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="761" data-end="815">
<p data-start="763" data-end="815">Bayesian program induction (sparse hypothesis space)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="816" data-end="872">
<p data-start="818" data-end="872">large language models (top-k/top-p next token pruning)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="874" data-end="1013">So instead of language being an unconstrained generator, it is a <strong data-start="939" data-end="992">highly compressed search space with strong priors</strong> supplied by context.</p>
<hr data-start="1015" data-end="1018">
<h1 data-start="1020" data-end="1073"><strong data-start="1022" data-end="1073">2. Context ≈ an implicit perceptual world model</strong></h1>
<p data-start="1074" data-end="1090">In your framing:</p>
<ul data-start="1092" data-end="1257">
<li data-start="1092" data-end="1122">
<p data-start="1094" data-end="1122">Context is not “text nearby”</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1123" data-end="1179">
<p data-start="1125" data-end="1179">It is a <strong data-start="1133" data-end="1179">latent world model shared between speakers</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="1180" data-end="1257">
<p data-start="1182" data-end="1257">Interpretation is constrained by what is <strong data-start="1223" data-end="1257">plausible in that shared model</strong></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1259" data-end="1280">So when someone says:</p>
<blockquote data-start="1282" data-end="1325">
<p data-start="1284" data-end="1325">“The council refused the permit because…”</p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start="1327" data-end="1395">We don’t spawn thousands of interpretations. We implicitly simulate:</p>
<ul data-start="1397" data-end="1489">
<li data-start="1397" data-end="1409">
<p data-start="1399" data-end="1409">the actors</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1410" data-end="1428">
<p data-start="1412" data-end="1428">their incentives</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1429" data-end="1456">
<p data-start="1431" data-end="1456">possible causal relations</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1457" data-end="1489">
<p data-start="1459" data-end="1489">typical institutional behavior</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1491" data-end="1614">This is <strong data-start="1499" data-end="1523">not stored in syntax</strong> but in <strong data-start="1531" data-end="1579">predictive schemas about how the world works</strong> that language activates on demand.</p>
<p data-start="1616" data-end="1724">So language doesn’t need to encode <em data-start="1651" data-end="1666">all reasoning</em>, it just needs to provide <strong data-start="1693" data-end="1723">handles into shared models</strong>.</p>
<hr data-start="1726" data-end="1729">
<h1 data-start="1731" data-end="1786"><strong data-start="1733" data-end="1786">3. Language is built from predictive proxy models</strong></h1>
<p data-start="1787" data-end="1826">This is the deepest part of your point.</p>
<p data-start="1828" data-end="1890">Rather than “words map to referents,” a more accurate view is:</p>
<ul data-start="1892" data-end="2052">
<li data-start="1892" data-end="1933">
<p data-start="1894" data-end="1933">Words evoke <strong data-start="1906" data-end="1933">lightweight simulations</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="1934" data-end="1985">
<p data-start="1936" data-end="1985">Phrases trigger <strong data-start="1952" data-end="1985">compressed dynamical patterns</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="1986" data-end="2052">
<p data-start="1988" data-end="2052">Meaning is the <strong data-start="2003" data-end="2038">unrolling of a predictive model</strong>, not a lookup</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2054" data-end="2189">So semantics are not spatially arranged in a symbolic ontology, but <strong data-start="2122" data-end="2188">embedded like a chart of likely temporal or causal transitions</strong>.</p>
<p data-start="2191" data-end="2203">This is why:</p>
<ul data-start="2205" data-end="2383">
<li data-start="2205" data-end="2231">
<p data-start="2207" data-end="2231">Related meanings cluster</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2232" data-end="2269">
<p data-start="2234" data-end="2269">Metaphor works by re-using dynamics</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2270" data-end="2299">
<p data-start="2272" data-end="2299">Syntax mirrors control flow</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2300" data-end="2338">
<p data-start="2302" data-end="2338">Narrative mirrors physical causality</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2339" data-end="2383">
<p data-start="2341" data-end="2383">Grammar mirrors agency and event structure</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2385" data-end="2466">Language is therefore not a static code but a <strong data-start="2431" data-end="2465">low-bandwidth executable model</strong>.</p>
<hr data-start="2468" data-end="2471">
<h1 data-start="2473" data-end="2535"><strong data-start="2475" data-end="2535">4. Semantic proximity becomes temporal/spatial proximity</strong></h1>
<p data-start="2536" data-end="2594">Your insight here is important and technically defensible:</p>
<p data-start="2596" data-end="2666">In corpora, meanings that occur near each other in reality tend to be:</p>
<ul data-start="2668" data-end="2827">
<li data-start="2668" data-end="2699">
<p data-start="2670" data-end="2699">mentioned in nearby sentences</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2700" data-end="2729">
<p data-start="2702" data-end="2729">linked by discourse markers</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2730" data-end="2757">
<p data-start="2732" data-end="2757">co-situated in narratives</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2758" data-end="2799">
<p data-start="2760" data-end="2799">ordered by causal or temporal relations</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2800" data-end="2827">
<p data-start="2802" data-end="2827">embedded in shared frames</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2829" data-end="2893">This makes language <strong data-start="2849" data-end="2859">metric</strong> in an information-geometry sense:</p>
<ul data-start="2895" data-end="3065">
<li data-start="2895" data-end="2936">
<p data-start="2897" data-end="2936">semantic distance ≈ contextual distance</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2937" data-end="2977">
<p data-start="2939" data-end="2977">related concepts ≈ short paths in text</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2978" data-end="3020">
<p data-start="2980" data-end="3020">inference ≈ traversing local connections</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3021" data-end="3065">
<p data-start="3023" data-end="3065">reasoning ≈ walking those paths coherently</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="3067" data-end="3140">This is exactly why embedding spaces, attention maps, and prompting work.</p>
<hr data-start="3142" data-end="3145">
<h1 data-start="3147" data-end="3184"><strong data-start="3149" data-end="3184">5. So the deeper claim becomes:</strong></h1>
<blockquote data-start="3186" data-end="3353">
<p data-start="3188" data-end="3353"><strong data-start="3188" data-end="3353">Language is a compressed predictive simulation lattice, shaped by shared world models, that enables sparse traversal of meaning rather than combinatorial search.</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start="3355" data-end="3374">Or in one sentence:</p>
<blockquote data-start="3376" data-end="3504">
<p data-start="3378" data-end="3504"><strong data-start="3378" data-end="3504">Language works because it encodes the world as a navigable approximation, not because it describes the world exhaustively.</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<hr data-start="3506" data-end="3509">
<h1 data-start="3511" data-end="3545"><strong data-start="3513" data-end="3545">6. Consequences of this view</strong></h1>
<h3 data-start="3547" data-end="3606">✅ Explains why next-token prediction yields reasoning</h3>
<p data-start="3607" data-end="3672">Because next token ≈ next state in a compressed world-simulation.</p>
<h3 data-start="3674" data-end="3710">✅ Explains in-context learning</h3>
<p data-start="3711" data-end="3795">Context loads a local subgraph of the world-model, which is then traversed sparsely.</p>
<h3 data-start="3797" data-end="3833">✅ Explains why LLMs generalize</h3>
<p data-start="3834" data-end="3949">They are not memorizing sentences; they are recovering the <strong data-start="3893" data-end="3948">transition geometry of a shared predictive manifold</strong>.</p>
<h3 data-start="3951" data-end="3992">✅ Explains fast human understanding</h3>
<p data-start="3993" data-end="4075">Humans prune interpretations by perceptual expectation, not by brute force search.</p>
<h3 data-start="4077" data-end="4114">✅ Explains ambiguity resolution</h3>
<p data-start="4115" data-end="4201">Disambiguation is elimination of impossible world trajectories, not syntactic parsing.</p>
<hr data-start="4203" data-end="4206">
<h1 data-start="4208" data-end="4270"><strong data-start="4210" data-end="4270">7. Where this departs from Barenholtz and pushes further</strong></h1>
<p data-start="4272" data-end="4303">You’re adding a key refinement:</p>
<div class="_tableContainer_1rjym_1"><div tabindex="-1" class="group _tableWrapper_1rjym_13 flex w-fit flex-col-reverse"><table data-start="4305" data-end="4690" class="w-fit min-w-(--thread-content-width)"><thead data-start="4305" data-end="4353"><tr data-start="4305" data-end="4353"><th data-start="4305" data-end="4334" data-col-size="sm">Barenholtz (broad reading)</th><th data-start="4334" data-end="4353" data-col-size="md">Your refinement</th></tr></thead><tbody data-start="4364" data-end="4690"><tr data-start="4364" data-end="4455"><td data-start="4364" data-end="4400" data-col-size="sm">Language is immanently generative</td><td data-start="4400" data-end="4455" data-col-size="md">Language is immanently <em data-start="4425" data-end="4453">predictive and model-based</em></td></tr><tr data-start="4456" data-end="4582"><td data-start="4456" data-end="4498" data-col-size="sm">Next-token learning ≈ language learning</td><td data-start="4498" data-end="4582" data-col-size="md">Next-token learning ≈ learning a world-conditioned simulator encoded in language</td></tr><tr data-start="4583" data-end="4690"><td data-start="4583" data-end="4624" data-col-size="sm">Syntax contains its own generative law</td><td data-start="4624" data-end="4690" data-col-size="md">Semantics contain the predictive constraints that prune syntax</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="sticky h-0 select-none end-(--thread-content-margin) self-end"><div class="absolute end-0 flex items-end"><span class="" data-state="closed"><button aria-label="Copy table" class="hover:bg-token-bg-tertiary text-token-text-secondary my-1 rounded-sm p-1 transition-opacity group-[:not(:hover):not(:focus-within)]:pointer-events-none group-[:not(:hover):not(:focus-within)]:opacity-0"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.468