# Mathematical formalization (compact)

### Setup — world, corpus, templates

* Let (S_t) be the latent world state at discrete time (t). Observed tokens (x_t\in\mathcal X) are emitted by agents observing/acting in the world:
  [
  x_t \sim p_{\mathrm{emit}}(x\mid S_t).
  ]

* The corpus distribution is the marginal over emitted token sequences:
  [
  p(x_{1:T})=\int p(S_{1:T})\prod_{t=1}^T p_{\mathrm{emit}}(x_t\mid S_t),dS_{1:T}.
  ]

* Let (\mathcal H) be a (possibly huge) set of **algorithmic templates** (programs, generative maps) (h) that map short histories to distributions over continuations. Each (h) induces a distribution (p_h(x_{t+1}\mid x_{\le t})).

* Assume the true corpus distribution can be approximated by a (sparse) mixture over a *small* active subset (H_\text{act}\subset \mathcal H) conditioned on context:
  [
  p(x_{t+1}\mid x_{\le t}) \approx \sum_{h\in H_\text{act}(x_{\le t})} p(h\mid x_{\le t}),p_h(x_{t+1}\mid x_{\le t}).
  ]

### Holographic / distributed-cue assumption

* There exist many weak cues (c) distributed across contexts such that log evidence accumulates additively:
  [
  \log p(h\mid x^{(1:n)}) = \log p(h) + \sum_{i=1}^n \log p(x^{(i)}\mid h) + \mathrm{const}.
  ]
* No single cue fully identifies (h), but the sum across many contexts concentrates the posterior (posterior concentration).

### Free-energy (predictive-coding) objective

* A learner maintains an approximate posterior (q(s)) over latent states or an approximate posterior (q(h)) over templates and minimizes predictive free energy:
  [
  \mathcal F(q) = \mathbb E_{q}[-\log p_{\mathrm{emit}}(x\mid s)] + \mathrm{KL}(q(s),|,p(s))
  ]
  or in template-space
  [
  \mathcal F(q) = -\mathbb E_{q}[\log p(x\mid h)] + \mathrm{KL}(q(h),|,p(h)).
  ]
  Minimizing (\mathcal F) yields the posterior (q^\star\propto p(h)p(x\mid h)), i.e. Bayesian update.

### Sparsity principle (operational)

* The learner uses a sparse approximate posterior (\tilde q) supported on (k) templates:
  [
  \tilde q(h) =
  \begin{cases}
  \propto p(h\mid x_{\le t}), &amp; h\in H_k\
  0, &amp; \text{otherwise}
  \end{cases}
  ]
  with (k\ll |\mathcal H|). Decision-making uses (\tilde q) to compute predictive marginals.

### Information-theoretic recoverability condition (sketch)

Let (\mathcal I(h; X_{1:N})) be mutual information between template (h) and (N)-token corpus. A sufficient condition for recoverability from a next-token objective with sample size (N) is:
[
\mathcal I(h; X_{1:N}) \gtrsim \log|\mathcal H_\text{dist}| + c,
]
where (|\mathcal H_\text{dist}|) is the number of distinguishable templates at the desired resolution and (c) is a small constant for estimation error. Intuition: the corpus must carry enough information to identify the template among alternatives.

# Mapping to transformer mechanics — equations and interpretation

We now show how a transformer’s components implement approximate posterior inference over templates.

### Representations and keys/values

* Token embeddings: (e_t\in\mathbb R^d).
* Keys (k_j = W_K e_j) and values (v_j = W_V e_j) stored for past positions (j\in{1,\dots,t}).
* Query at the current position (q_t = W_Q e_t).

Interpretation: each past position (j) encodes a *local evidence fragment* for certain templates; (k_j) is the address (sufficient statistic), (v_j) is the evidence-bearing content (e.g., parameters of local predictives or sufficient statistics that will shift posterior).

### Attention as soft posterior

* Attention weights:
  [
  \alpha_{tj} = \frac{\exp(q_t\cdot k_j / \tau)}{\sum_{j'} \exp(q_t\cdot k_{j'} / \tau)}.
  ]
  Take (\tau=1) for simplicity.

**Claim (informal derivation):** if (q_t\cdot k_j \approx \log p(\text{evidence at } j\mid h)) up to a template-dependent affine transform, then (\alpha_{tj}) approximates the normalized posterior mass over evidence positions — i.e. attention behaves like normalized likelihoods / posterior contributions.

**More concrete mapping.** Suppose there are discrete template hypotheses (h), and each past position (j) is generated (noisily) by some template-specific likelihood (p_j(x_j\mid h)). Let the model encode log-likelihood contribution as
[
q_t\cdot k_j \approx \log p_j(x_j\mid h^\star) + \beta(h^\star,q_t) + C_j,
]
where (\beta) is a slowly varying bias. Then exponentiating and normalizing computes a soft normalized weight proportional to the likelihood contributions — i.e. an attention-weighted likelihood aggregator. Aggregating values:
[
\tilde v_t = \sum_j \alpha_{tj}, v_j
]
is a context-conditioned summary that approximates a sufficient statistic for the posterior over templates. The final MLP/head converts (\tilde v_t) into logits — implementing the predictive marginal:
[
\hat p(x_{t+1}\mid x_{\le t}) \approx \mathrm{softmax}\big( W_{\mathrm{out}}(\mathrm{MLP}(\tilde v_t))\big).
]

### Attention as kernelized inference

Define a kernel
[
K(q_t, k_j) \propto \exp(q_t\cdot k_j).
]
Then attention computes a kernel-weighted average of the stored values. This is equivalent to nonparametric conditional density estimation or Nadaraya–Watson regression in feature space: the model retrieves examples similar to current context and reuses their continuation statistics. If keys encode template-sufficient statistics and values encode continuation distributions, attention implements an approximate mixture-of-experts posterior marginalization.

### Sparse posterior approximation via top-k/top-p &amp; attention sharpening

* In practice, the softmax will concentrate mass on a small subset of positions (effective sparsity). Early in learning the distribution may be broad; later, keys/queries refine so (\alpha) is sharply peaked on positions providing strongest evidence for a small set of templates — this is the sparse hypothesis selection.

### Residual stacks ≈ iterative refinement (emulating recursion)

Stacked transformer layers with residual connections provide repeated nonlinear transforms of (\tilde v_t). This can implement iterative inference: each layer refines the approximate posterior / summary statistic, much like variational iterative updates or an unrolled inference algorithm. Different heads can route different sub-templates (e.g., control-flow, stack pointer, local memory).

# Short derivation: attention as single-step Bayes (toy)

Assume a discrete hypothesis set ({h}). Each past position (j) provides evidence with likelihood (L_j(h)=p(x_j\mid h)). Suppose the model encodes (\log L_j(h)) linearly in (k_j) and (q_t):
[
q_t\cdot k_j \approx \log L_j(h^\star) + \mathrm{const}.
