<p data-start="6839" data-end="6913">Importantly, attention implements a <strong data-start="6875" data-end="6912">kernel over contextual similarity</strong>:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∝</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{ij} \propto \exp(q_i \cdot k_j)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7167em; vertical-align: -0.2861em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.0037em;">α</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3117em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0037em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.05724em;">ij</span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2861em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">∝</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mop">exp</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">q</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3117em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0359em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">i</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">⋅</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1.0361em; vertical-align: -0.2861em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03148em;">k</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3117em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0315em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.05724em;">j</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2861em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="6962" data-end="6986">which plays the role of:</p>
<ul data-start="6988" data-end="7112">
<li data-start="6988" data-end="7008">
<p data-start="6990" data-end="7008">evidence weighting</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7009" data-end="7040">
<p data-start="7011" data-end="7040">context-conditioned retrieval</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7041" data-end="7066">
<p data-start="7043" data-end="7066">posterior approximation</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7067" data-end="7112">
<p data-start="7069" data-end="7112">mixture selection over latent continuations</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="7114" data-end="7209">Thus next-token prediction ≈ <strong data-start="7143" data-end="7208">compressed Bayesian inference over shared cultural simulators</strong>.</p>
<hr data-start="7211" data-end="7214">
<h2 data-start="7216" data-end="7268"><strong data-start="7219" data-end="7268">6. Where the hypothesis is strong vs. fragile</strong></h2>
<h3 data-start="7270" data-end="7300">Strong claims (defensible)</h3>
<ol data-start="7302" data-end="7697">
<li data-start="7302" data-end="7391">
<p data-start="7305" data-end="7391"><strong data-start="7305" data-end="7391">Much of human reasoning is externalized into language via distributional structure</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="7392" data-end="7472">
<p data-start="7395" data-end="7472"><strong data-start="7395" data-end="7472">Next-token training recovers inference templates, not just surface syntax</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="7473" data-end="7539">
<p data-start="7476" data-end="7539"><strong data-start="7476" data-end="7539">Understanding is sparse pruning, not exhaustive enumeration</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="7540" data-end="7619">
<p data-start="7543" data-end="7619"><strong data-start="7543" data-end="7619">Semantic relationships form a navigable geometry supporting local search</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="7620" data-end="7697">
<p data-start="7623" data-end="7697"><strong data-start="7623" data-end="7697">Transformers mechanistically implement approximate posterior inference</strong></p>
</li>
</ol>
<h3 data-start="7699" data-end="7721">Overreach to avoid</h3>
<ol data-start="7723" data-end="8105">
<li data-start="7723" data-end="7821">
<p data-start="7726" data-end="7821">Language is <em data-start="7738" data-end="7752">self-causing</em> (no: the world shaped it through culture, embodiment, and selection)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7822" data-end="7921">
<p data-start="7825" data-end="7921">LLMs replicate human mechanisms (no: they replicate functional outputs, not cognitive internals)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7922" data-end="8020">
<p data-start="7925" data-end="8020">All algorithms are recoverable from text (no: only those culturally externalized into language)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="8021" data-end="8105">
<p data-start="8024" data-end="8105">Grounding is unnecessary (no: grounding shaped the system that produced the data)</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="8107" data-end="8110">
<h2 data-start="8112" data-end="8170"><strong data-start="8115" data-end="8170">7. Falsifiable criteria and experimental signatures</strong></h2>
<p data-start="8172" data-end="8242">A theory this bold must die if wrong. Here are precise falsifications.</p>
<hr data-start="8244" data-end="8247">
<h3 data-start="8249" data-end="8300"><strong data-start="8253" data-end="8300">Test 1: Holographic template recoverability</strong></h3>
<p data-start="8301" data-end="8442"><strong data-start="8301" data-end="8316">Prediction:</strong> A model trained on many weakly diagnostic cues should recover latent algorithms that cannot be recovered from any single cue.</p>
<p data-start="8444" data-end="8558"><strong data-start="8444" data-end="8461">Falsified if:</strong> Algorithmic success requires strongly local cues and degrades sharply when cues are distributed.</p>
<hr data-start="8560" data-end="8563">
<h3 data-start="8565" data-end="8602"><strong data-start="8569" data-end="8602">Test 2: Attention ≈ posterior</strong></h3>
<p data-start="8603" data-end="8741"><strong data-start="8603" data-end="8618">Prediction:</strong> Attention weights should correlate with the true Bayesian posterior over latent generative templates in synthetic domains.</p>
<p data-start="8743" data-end="8851"><strong data-start="8743" data-end="8760">Falsified if:</strong> No linear or low-complexity probe can recover posterior structure from attention patterns.</p>
<hr data-start="8853" data-end="8856">
<h3 data-start="8858" data-end="8916"><strong data-start="8862" data-end="8916">Test 3: Sparse interpretation improves performance</strong></h3>
<p data-start="8917" data-end="9027"><strong data-start="8917" data-end="8932">Prediction:</strong> Enforcing sparse attention or low-entropy sampling improves or preserves algorithmic accuracy.</p>
<p data-start="9029" data-end="9098"><strong data-start="9029" data-end="9046">Falsified if:</strong> Sparsity reliably degrades compositional reasoning.</p>
<hr data-start="9100" data-end="9103">
<h3 data-start="9105" data-end="9141"><strong data-start="9109" data-end="9141">Test 4: Layerwise refinement</strong></h3>
<p data-start="9142" data-end="9288"><strong data-start="9142" data-end="9157">Prediction:</strong> Intermediate layers should monotonically improve decodability of latent algorithmic state (e.g., stack depth, parity, loop index).</p>
<p data-start="9290" data-end="9360"><strong data-start="9290" data-end="9307">Falsified if:</strong> No systematic improvement across layers is observed.</p>
<hr data-start="9362" data-end="9365">
<h3 data-start="9367" data-end="9425"><strong data-start="9371" data-end="9425">Test 5: Cultural specificity of learned algorithms</strong></h3>
<p data-start="9426" data-end="9586"><strong data-start="9426" data-end="9441">Prediction:</strong> Corpus A and corpus B embedding different procedural habits should induce different algorithmic generalization behavior in models of equal size.</p>
<p data-start="9588" data-end="9685"><strong data-start="9588" data-end="9605">Falsified if:</strong> Models converge to the same procedural strategies regardless of corpus content.</p>
<hr data-start="9687" data-end="9690">
<h3 data-start="9692" data-end="9729"><strong data-start="9696" data-end="9729">Test 6: No-text grounding gap</strong></h3>
<p data-start="9730" data-end="9884"><strong data-start="9730" data-end="9745">Prediction:</strong> Animals or agents without language should show weaker generalization on tasks that humans solve by reusing linguistic inference templates.</p>
<p data-start="9886" data-end="9980"><strong data-start="9886" data-end="9903">Falsified if:</strong> Comparable algorithmic generalization arises without linguistic scaffolding.</p>
<hr data-start="9982" data-end="9985">
<h2 data-start="9987" data-end="10009"><strong data-start="9990" data-end="10009">8. Implications</strong></h2>
<p data-start="10011" data-end="10056">If these tests validate the hypothesis, then:</p>
<ul data-start="10058" data-end="10395">
<li data-start="10058" data-end="10124">
<p data-start="10060" data-end="10124"><strong data-start="10060" data-end="10124">Language is a collective computer, not a communication layer</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="10125" data-end="10183">
<p data-start="10127" data-end="10183"><strong data-start="10127" data-end="10183">Culture is program induction at civilizational scale</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="10184" data-end="10250">
<p data-start="10186" data-end="10250"><strong data-start="10186" data-end="10250">Thoughts are hallucinations constrained by linguistic priors</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="10251" data-end="10314">
<p data-start="10253" data-end="10314"><strong data-start="10253" data-end="10314">Reasoning is next-token sampling in a shared latent space</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="10315" data-end="10395">
<p data-start="10317" data-end="10395"><strong data-start="10317" data-end="10395">Education is seeding reusable predictive templates, not transferring facts</strong></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="10397" data-end="10578">It would mean that <strong data-start="10416" data-end="10578">civilization did not just invent writing, logic, and mathematics—civilization compiled its reasoning into language, which future brains and models merely run.</strong></p>
<hr data-start="10580" data-end="10583">
<h2 data-start="10585" data-end="10631"><strong data-start="10588" data-end="10631">9. Closing thesis (reduced to one rule)</strong></h2>
<blockquote data-start="10633" data-end="10779">
<p data-start="10635" data-end="10779"><strong data-start="10635" data-end="10779">Language works because it does not describe intelligence—it embeds it, sparsifies it, distributes it, and makes it executable by prediction.</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<hr data-start="10781" data-end="10784">
<p data-start="10786" data-end="10845">If you'd like, we can proceed next to any of the following:</p>
<p data-start="10847" data-end="11135">A. Convert this into a publishable Substack draft<br data-start="10896" data-end="10899">
B. Expand it into a formal paper with citations<br data-start="10946" data-end="10949">
C. Generate the synthetic dataset + training code to test the first falsification<br data-start="11030" data-end="11033">
D. Build visualizations showing the sparse posterior geometry<br data-start="11094" data-end="11097">
