<p data-start="338" data-end="428">This is a <em data-start="348" data-end="370">complete paper draft</em>, ready for expansion into LaTeX or submission draft form.</p>
<hr data-start="430" data-end="433">
<h1 data-start="435" data-end="586"><strong data-start="437" data-end="586">Language as a Holographic Predictive Medium: Distributed Algorithmic Templates, Sparse Inference, and Emergent Computation in Next-Token Learners</strong></h1>
<h3 data-start="588" data-end="604"><strong data-start="592" data-end="604">Abstract</strong></h3>
<p data-start="606" data-end="1689">The emergent capabilities of large language models (LLMs) demonstrate that optimizing for next-token prediction over natural language corpora is sufficient to recover reasoning, planning, recursion, and algorithmic generalization without explicit symbolic architectures or external reward. We advance the hypothesis that natural language functions as a <em data-start="959" data-end="1002">holographically encoded predictive medium</em>—a distributed substrate embedding reusable algorithmic templates that can be retrieved via sparse inference. In this view, comprehension is model selection over latent computational programs, semantics form a navigable geometry induced by contextual transitions, and transformer attention approximates posterior inference over candidate generative templates. We formalize the hypothesis, connect it to predictive processing, Bayesian program induction, and information geometry, and derive testable predictions with concrete falsification strategies. We conclude that language is not merely a vehicle for expressing thought but a lossy, shared execution surface for cultural algorithms.</p>
<hr data-start="1691" data-end="1694">
<h2 data-start="1696" data-end="1718"><strong data-start="1699" data-end="1718">1. Introduction</strong></h2>
<p data-start="1720" data-end="2258">Recent scaling results have revealed a surprising empirical fact: optimizing transformer models solely for autoregressive next-token prediction over text induces qualitatively broad reasoning capabilities, including mathematical inference, hypothetical reasoning, procedural generalization, analogical transfer, and implicit world modeling (Brown et al., 2020; Kaplan et al., 2020; Wei et al., 2022). These capabilities emerge without explicit symbolic supervision, structured logical form, embodied interaction, or reinforcement signals.</p>
<p data-start="2260" data-end="2533">This challenges dominant theoretical assumptions across linguistics and cognitive science that treat language as a communicative surface code downstream of a richer internal reasoning system (Chomsky, 1957; Fodor, 1975). Instead, we explore the alternative hypothesis that:</p>
<blockquote data-start="2535" data-end="2758">
<p data-start="2537" data-end="2758"><strong data-start="2537" data-end="2758">Human language encodes reusable generative algorithms in a distributed, compressed, and probabilistically recoverable form, such that next-token prediction performs implicit inference over cultural cognitive programs.</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start="2760" data-end="3213">This view synthesizes ideas from predictive processing (Friston, 2010; Clark, 2013), Bayesian concept learning (Tenenbaum et al., 2011), cultural iterated learning (Kirby, 2001), and distributional semantics (Harris, 1954; Mikolov et al., 2013), but departs from each by proposing a stronger computational claim: <strong data-start="3073" data-end="3144">language is not merely descriptive nor statistical, but operational</strong>—a low-bandwidth, shared execution surface for distributed inference.</p>
<hr data-start="3215" data-end="3218">
<h2 data-start="3220" data-end="3242"><strong data-start="3223" data-end="3242">2. Related Work</strong></h2>
<h3 data-start="3244" data-end="3301">2.1 Distributional Semantics and Emergent Structure</h3>
<p data-start="3302" data-end="3608">Distributional approaches demonstrate that semantic similarity emerges from context statistics (Harris, 1954; Mikolov et al., 2013; Pennington et al., 2014). Transformer models further show that complex cognitive behaviors emerge from scaling sequence prediction (Vaswani et al., 2017; Brown et al., 2020).</p>
<h3 data-start="3610" data-end="3661">2.2 Predictive Coding and Cognitive Inference</h3>
<p data-start="3662" data-end="3916">Predictive processing frameworks model perception and cognition as inference under a generative model minimizing prediction error (Friston, 2010; Clark, 2013; Hohwy, 2013), suggesting linguistic comprehension may optimize over internal predictive models.</p>
<h3 data-start="3918" data-end="3953">2.3 Bayesian Program Learning</h3>
<p data-start="3954" data-end="4217">Humans infer compositional latent programs from sparse data (Tenenbaum et al., 2011; Lake et al., 2015), raising the possibility that next-token learners recover implicit program templates encoded across language distributions rather than explicit symbolic rules.</p>
<h3 data-start="4219" data-end="4271">2.4 Iterated Learning and Cultural Compression</h3>
<p data-start="4272" data-end="4469">Languages evolve to optimize transmission of structure under learning constraints (Kirby, 2001; Tamariz &amp; Kirby, 2015), implying that grammar may adapt to encode cultural priors in compressed form.</p>
<hr data-start="4471" data-end="4474">
<h2 data-start="4476" data-end="4501"><strong data-start="4479" data-end="4501">3. Core Hypothesis</strong></h2>
<p data-start="4503" data-end="4542">We articulate five interlocking claims:</p>
<ol data-start="4544" data-end="5246">
<li data-start="4544" data-end="4663">
<p data-start="4547" data-end="4663"><strong data-start="4547" data-end="4570">Template Hypothesis</strong> — Cultural reasoning strategies are encoded as <em data-start="4618" data-end="4650">latent computational templates</em> in language.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4664" data-end="4806">
<p data-start="4667" data-end="4806"><strong data-start="4667" data-end="4691">Holographic Encoding</strong> — No single sample expresses a full template; templates are recoverable via distributed weak cues across contexts.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4807" data-end="4955">
<p data-start="4810" data-end="4955"><strong data-start="4810" data-end="4830">Sparse Inference</strong> — Comprehension involves pruning a posterior over templates to a small candidate set rather than exhaustive semantic search.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4956" data-end="5077">
<p data-start="4959" data-end="5077"><strong data-start="4959" data-end="4980">Semantic Geometry</strong> — Contextual adjacency induces a metric over meanings that enables local inference by traversal.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5078" data-end="5246">
<p data-start="5081" data-end="5246"><strong data-start="5081" data-end="5111">Mechanistic Recoverability</strong> — Transformer attention approximates posterior inference over templates, and layer dynamics implement iterative hypothesis refinement.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="5248" data-end="5251">
<h2 data-start="5253" data-end="5276"><strong data-start="5256" data-end="5276">4. Formalization</strong></h2>
<p data-start="5278" data-end="5527">Let <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="script">H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h \in \mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7335em; vertical-align: -0.0391em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">h</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">∈</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.00965em;">H</span></span></span></span> be a latent generative template encoding an algorithmic inference pattern. A corpus <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1:T}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.5806em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3283em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mtight">1</span><span class="mrel mtight">:</span><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">T</span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> is produced by emission from latent state sequences <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_t</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.5806em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">s</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2806em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">t</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> whose transitions are governed implicitly by these templates:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><munderover><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x_{1:T}) = \int p(s_{1:T} \mid h) \prod_{t=1}^{T} p(x_t \mid s_t) \, ds_{1:T}.</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3283em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mtight">1</span><span class="mrel mtight">:</span><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">T</span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 2.2222em; vertical-align: -0.8622em;"></span><span class="mop op-symbol large-op" style="margin-right: 0.44445em; position: relative; top: -0.0011em;">∫</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">s</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3283em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mtight">1</span><span class="mrel mtight">:</span><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">T</span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">∣</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 3.0954em; vertical-align: -1.2671em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">h</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mop op-limits"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 1.8283em;"><span style="top: -1.8829em; margin-left: 0em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3.05em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">t</span><span class="mrel mtight">=</span><span class="mord mtight">1</span></span></span></span><span style="top: -3.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3.05em;"></span><span><span class="m