<li data-start="483" data-end="540">
<p data-start="486" data-end="540"><strong data-start="486" data-end="505">Substack essays</strong> (as online publications with date)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="541" data-end="635">
<p data-start="544" data-end="635"><strong data-start="544" data-end="565">Talks or lectures</strong> (as “presentations” or “unpublished lectures” with URLs if available)</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="637" data-end="640">
<h3 data-start="642" data-end="729">✅ Here is a clean, academically valid citation entry you can paste into your paper:</h3>
<h4 data-start="731" data-end="786"><strong data-start="736" data-end="786">APA-style reference (for the end of the paper)</strong></h4>
<blockquote data-start="788" data-end="1072">
<p data-start="790" data-end="1072"><strong data-start="790" data-end="808">Barenholtz, E.</strong> (2024). <em data-start="817" data-end="910">Language as an Immanent Generative System: Why Next-Token Learning Recovers Human Reasoning</em> [Working manuscript]. Substack.<br data-start="942" data-end="945">
<em data-start="947" data-end="1072">Used with reference to associated public lectures on the generative structure of language and algorithmic recovery in LLMs.</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start="1074" data-end="1163"><em data-start="1074" data-end="1163">(Replace 2024 with 2023/2025 depending on the actual publication date when finalizing.)</em></p>
<hr data-start="1165" data-end="1168">
<h3 data-start="1170" data-end="1219">✅ In-text citations to add to the paper draft</h3>
<p data-start="1221" data-end="1283">You can insert these into the introduction or framing section:</p>
<blockquote data-start="1285" data-end="1558">
<p data-start="1287" data-end="1558"><em data-start="1287" data-end="1558">Recent work argues that natural language uniquely functions as an “immanent generative system,” where the rule of language is simply its own continuation, enabling next-token learners to recover structured reasoning without external syntactic priors (Barenholtz, 2024).</em></p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote data-start="1560" data-end="1871">
<p data-start="1562" data-end="1871"><em data-start="1562" data-end="1871">This view proposes that cultural and algorithmic priors become holographically embedded in linguistic distributions, such that textual continuation alone suffices to reconstruct procedural inference templates, without requiring explicit symbolic encodings or innate grammatical machinery (Barenholtz, 2024).</em></p>
</blockquote>
