8 9.17239 3.6708 8.50176 4.49805 8.50159Z"></path><path d="M10.0003 8.50159C10.8276 8.50176 11.4982 9.17239 11.4984 9.99963C11.4984 10.827 10.8277 11.4985 10.0003 11.4987C9.17283 11.4987 8.50131 10.8271 8.50131 9.99963C8.50149 9.17228 9.17294 8.50159 10.0003 8.50159Z"></path></svg></button></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none [--shadow-height:45px] has-data-writing-block:pointer-events-none has-data-writing-block:-mt-(--shadow-height) has-data-writing-block:pt-(--shadow-height) [&amp;:has([data-writing-block])&gt;*]:pointer-events-auto scroll-mt-(--header-height)" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="bbb212ac-8a71-4ddb-9d0d-3c044b8f8744" data-testid="conversation-turn-17" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="user"><h5 class="sr-only">You said:</h5><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto pt-12 [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] thread-sm:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] thread-lg:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] thread-lg:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="user" data-message-id="bbb212ac-8a71-4ddb-9d0d-3c044b8f8744" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-1"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden items-end rtl:items-start"><div class="user-message-bubble-color relative rounded-[18px] px-4 py-1.5 data-[multiline]:py-3 max-w-[var(--user-chat-width,70%)]" data-multiline=""><div class="whitespace-pre-wrap">No you can quote The Brain as a Language Model (But Not How You Think)
The explosion of large language models (LLMs) has given us a powerful new framework for intelligence. These systems exhibit full linguistic competence based on learning to perform the deceptively simple task of predicting the next token in a sequence. They accomplish this through two key mechanisms: first, by using rich representations of previous sequences that allow each prediction to be conditioned on the full context window; second, by learning multi-layered representations that enable them to generalize far beyond their training data, extracting deep structural patterns that handle virtually any novel linguistic context.

But how does next-token prediction lead to long, coherent sentences, paragraphs, and sophisticated reasoning? The key insight is that what LLMs actually learn is not just individual token continuations but how broader sequences tend to unfold—enabling them to generate coherent, “smooth” future continuations.

Consider how the model might handle these two scenarios and consider what the very next word might be:

“Emma had been looking everywhere for her missing car keys. After checking the couch cushions and her purse, she finally looked in her jacket pocket and found...”

“Emma was just cleaning out her grandmother’s basement when she moved an old trunk and found...”

In the first case, both you and ChatGPT would likely generate “them” (maybe followed by ‘right where she had left them’ or maybe just a period) while the second case would favor “an“ (followed by who knows what?). Crucially, in plotting this minor next word, the system seems to be anticipating what will come later based on what came before, though not immediately before, which was identical. The choice between these simple articles reflects the model’s implicit understanding of how these different narrative paths will likely unfold across many subsequent tokens.

This is what allows LLMs to exhibit what can seem like an ability to think well in advance of just the next token. What they have really learned are paths through the embedding surface, routes through representational space that emerge from two distinct but complementary learned structures: the embedding weights that position tokens in semantic/syntactic neighborhoods, and the attention weights that determine how contextual information flows between these positions. Together, these learned structures guide which regions become accessible given the current context. These paths represent coherent ways that sequences can unfold. In the example above, a “goal-directed search path” flows toward regions associated with specific objects and successful completion, while an “accidental discovery path” moves toward areas associated with surprise and open possibilities. The choice of a given token is thus the choice of a given path, and the rest of the sequence unfolds autoregressively along that trajectory. This kind of learning, where the choice of the next token reflects an implicit commitment to a long-range trajectory, is the fundamental computational breakthrough of autoregressive architectures.

I have recently proposed that this autoregressive structure may serve as a computational framework not only for human language but for broader cognitive architecture. In this view, cognition consists of a generative process in which previous states—perceptual, linguistic, motor—are used to infer the next useful cognitive state. These tokens can include motor commands, attentional shifts, fragments of inner speech, or visual imagery, each generated to serve the ongoing behavioral sequence effectively. All emerge from the brain’s learned ability to generate appropriate next states by navigating trajectories through cognitive token space, paths that are shaped by the deep sequential structure of past experience. This creates a continuous autoregressive loop where the system’s motor outputs produce sensory feedback (proprioceptive, visual, auditory) that becomes input for subsequent processing, allowing trajectories to unfold through a series of contextually appropriate, utility-enhancing cognitive tokens.

In a nutshell, cognition can be understood to involve an autoregressive loop where sensory input gets processed into cognitive tokens, attention mechanisms weight the influence of recent cognitive history, and the system generates the next appropriate cognitive state based on learned trajectory patterns. These next cognitive states can include both internal mental operations—visual imagery, inner speech, motor plans, memory retrievals—as well as expressed behavioral outputs through muscle activations. The next cognitive state emerges from both this generation process and ongoing sensory inputs. Note that these sensory inputs are themselves the result of previous motor outputs + the world, creating a closed loop where the system’s own actions continuously reshape its input stream.

This framework provides a computationally tractable account of cognition that collapses the classical array of cognitive modules into a single unified generative process: long-term memory is the learned weights that shape the trajectory manifold; short-term/working memory is the residual activation of the previous sequence’s representation, i.e. the “context”; attention is the selective weighting process that determines which aspects of the contextual sequence most strongly influence the generation of the next cognitive token.

Okay, but how does it get that way?
A critical question in relation to this theory is how the high-dimensional manifold is shaped such that resulting trajectories are functionally useful. In other words, how does the system learn? In LLMs, this problem is solved through a supervised framework: the model’s next-token predictions are compared to fixed text corpora that provide explicit targets at each generative step based on the previous sequence. Similarly, the influentiao Predictive Coding framework relies on supervised learning through the incoming sensory stream, where the “correct answers” are continuously provided by environmental input; the system generates predictions about what sensory data will arrive next, then uses the actual incoming sensory signals as ground truth to compute prediction errors and update its internal models. This creates a form of moment-by-moment supervision where the world itself serves as the teacher, constantly correcting the brain’s predictions about sensory experience.

The lure of predictive frameworks is that they provide a clear learning objective. In the case of language, this approach works perfectly: the perceptual states (words/tokens) and the actions (generating words/tokens) are one and the same. The entire task is to generate linguistic sequences, so predicting the next token is equivalent to producing the correct action. But in general cognition, the perceptual states—the things you can observe—are precisely not the things you want to generate. Instead, an organism must generate actions that result in the next favorable state, then the next appropriate action, until some final action yields a positive outcome.

This reveals an awkward aspect of predictive coding: it requires the brain to maintain an entire parallel prediction system that outputs sensory forecasts, even though these predictions are not what the organism actually needs to generate. The point of cognition, of course, is to produce adaptive actions that lead to positive outcomes, not to accurately predict sensory input. The autoregressive framework avoids this conceptual inversion entirely.

Hebbian Learning and Trajectory Patterns
Instead of maintaining separate prediction and action systems, I propose that the cognitive system can learn directly from the state-action trajectories it experiences through basic Hebbian temporal association.

In supervised learning settings like LLM training, there is a “correct” action at each step (the next token), which provides explicit supervision. Biological cognition, however, operates within the classical reinforcement learning structure: extended chains of state-action pairs with only intermittent feedback at distant endpoints. This creates the fundamental temporal credit assignment problem.

The key insight from autoregressive architectures is that it is possible to solve this problem by learning trajectory-level representations rather than individual step-by-step mappings. Through autoregressive training on long sequences, LLMs learn trajectory patterns that capture extended dependencies and structural regularities in language

This same principle applies to biological cognition. The cognitive system learns state-action trajectory patterns through pure Hebbian temporal association:

P(st+1​∣st​,at​) = frequency of (st​,at​,st+1​) co-occurrences

When cognitive state st​ is followed by action at​, which leads to state st+1​, the neural pathways connecting these elements are strengthened through simple temporal co-occurrence. This learning requires no external teacher, and critically, no dedicated prediction-minus-input comparator required by PC. This is a biologically lean mechanism for building the world-in-action map.

Through repeated exposure to extended sequences, the system naturally discovers longer-range, abstract trajectory patterns (e.g., “attending before reaching” vs. “reaching without visual engagement”). This trajectory-level learning creates a comprehensive map of possible state-action pathways and their likely consequences.

An important caveat in the comparison between LLMs and biological cognition: The standard transformer architecture preserves the entire sequence within its context window (though some chat models likely use compression techniques for longer conversations). In biological systems, however, compression of the ‘context’ is likely occurring continuously and across multiple timescales, such that the entire contextual sequence over minutes, hours, or perhaps even longer periods serves to bias current generation through hierarchical compression where recent events are represented in fine detail while older experiences contribute as increasingly abstract contextual biases that shape current behavioral trajectories.

Reinforcement Biases the Trajectory
The trajectory patterns learned through unsupervised Hebbian association provide the shape of the behavioral space, a map of what tends to happen. But they don’t provide insight into which paths are adaptive.

This is where reinforcement learning (RL) becomes essential, not as a mechanism for learning individual state-action values, but as a system for evaluating and biasing entire learned trajectory patterns.

The temporal credit assignment problem dissolves because the unit of evaluation becomes the trajectory pattern itself. When a behavioral sequence culminates in a positive outcome, the RL system reinforces the entire learned pattern (e.g., “visual attention → postural adjustment → arm extension → grip preparation → contact”) as a coherent unit.

This approach leverages the trajectory-level representation already built by Hebbian learning, using RL to sculpt the manifold toward expressing patterns that prove functionally adaptive while avoiding those that lead to poor outcomes.

This raises a crucial architectural question: how exactly does RL bias trajectory selection toward adaptive outcomes? Two fundamentally different approaches are possible, each with distinct implications for cognitive flexibility and biological plausibility.

In one approach, RL operates by permanently modifying the learned autoregressive surface itself. Successful behavioral trajectories become strengthened by adjusting the synaptic weights that encode transition probabilities, making adaptive pathways intrinsically more likely to be generated in similar future contexts. This is analogous to how Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) modifies transformer weights to bias language models toward generating preferred response types.

Under this architecture, the infant’s successful reaching sequences would gradually reshape the underlying manifold, making the neural pathways connecting “visual attention → motor preparation → successful contact” stronger and more probable. The learned structure itself evolves to favor adaptive patterns, with the most reinforced trajectories becoming the “default” pathways that emerge most readily from similar starting contexts.

Alternatively, RL could provide dynamic control signals that bias trajectory selection during active generation without permanently altering the underlying structural knowledge. This would preserve the learned manifold of possible transitions while allowing flexible, context-dependent selection among pathways based on current goals, environmental demands, and situational factors.

In this architecture, the basic trajectory patterns learned through Hebbian association remain intact as a comprehensive map of behavioral possibilities. But during real-time generation, executive control systems (potentially involving the parietal attention networks discussed earlier) evaluate multiple possible continuations and apply goal-dependent biasing signals that favor trajectories aligned with current behavioral objectives. The same reaching manifold could support both gentle object exploration and rapid defensive movements, depending on contextual control signals.

These architectures offer different advantages. Weight-based modification provides computational efficiency as adaptive behaviors become automatic and don’t require ongoing control overhead. But real-time selection offers superior flexibility because the same learned structure can support diverse goals without requiring extensive retraining.

The biological reality may involve both mechanisms operating at different timescales: gradual weight modification for frequently reinforced patterns combined with real-time biasing for novel situations or competing goals. This hybrid approach would capture the benefits of both architectural strategies while remaining computationally tractable.

The Complete Autoregressive Cognitive Architecture
This autoregressive trajectory learning framework offers a unified computational account of both artificial and biological intelligence, grounded in a single elegant principle: learning what comes next in the context of what came before. To see how this framework integrates all the components we’ve discussed, including unsupervised structure learning, reinforcement-guided trajectory selection, and real-time cognitive generation, let’s trace the complete cognitive loop:

Raw Sensory Input: Environmental stimuli hit sensory receptors—photons on retina, pressure waves in cochlea, proprioceptive signals from muscles. Crucially, much of this sensory input results from the organism’s own previous motor outputs, creating a self-generated data stream.

Sensory Processing → Cognitive Tokens: Early sensory processing transforms raw signals into processed representations, which combine with current motor states, attention settings, and memory activations to form “cognitive tokens”, the basic units of the autoregressive sequence.

Contextual Embedding: Current cognitive tokens get embedded alongside recent cognitive history, creating rich sequential context that spans multiple timescales through hierarchical compression—detailed recent events, abstract longer-term patterns.

Self-Attention Weighting: Distributed attention mechanisms (likely involving parietal control systems) weight how much each element in this cognitive sequence influences next token generation. Recent motor consequences, earlier behavioral contexts, and current environmental demands all contribute to this dynamic weighting process.

Trajectory Path Selection: Here, both unsupervised structure learning and reinforcement learning converge. The learned manifold provides possible paths consisting of all the ways cognitive sequences can coherently unfold based on temporal contingencies. But RL-derived value signals bias this selection toward trajectories that have previously led to adaptive outcomes. The system isn’t just generating probable next states; it’s selecting among learned pathways based on their functional consequences.

Next Cognitive Token Generation: The system generates the next cognitive token by following the selected trajectory path. These tokens encompass both internal mental operations (visual imagery, inner speech, motor planning, memory retrieval) and expressed behavioral outputs (muscle activations that produce environmental actions). Both are part of the same autoregressive sequence.

Environmental and Internal Consequences: Motor outputs immediately alter the sensory input stream—arm movements create proprioceptive feedback and visual flow, vocalizations produce auditory feedback. Simultaneously, internal cognitive tokens create persistent internal state changes that become part of the ongoing cognitive context.

Loop Continuation: The next cognitive state emerges from the combination of new sensory inputs (which results from motor actions plus the sensed environment) and ongoing internally generated cognitive states. This integrated sensory-cognitive context becomes input for the subsequent cycle, perpetuating a continuous loop where both external consequences and internal mental states shape what the system generates next.

The remarkable aspect of this architecture is its parsimony: a single autoregressive mechanism, guided by learned trajectory patterns and biased by evaluative feedback, can account for the full spectrum of intelligent behavior, from basic sensorimotor coordination to complex planning, from perceptual processing to abstract reasoning. The apparent “magic” of sophisticated cognition emerges from the same computational principles that enable language models to generate coherent text, scaled to the domain of embodied action in a structured world.

This framework suggests that the fundamental breakthrough of large language models—learning deep sequential structure through autoregressive generation—reveals core principles of intelligence itself. Whether the tokens are words in a linguistic sequence or cognitive states in a behavioral sequence, the same computational logic applies: learn the statistical structure of extended sequences, then use evaluative feedback to navigate that learned structure toward functionally successful outcomes. The brain’s capacity for flexible, adaptive behavior in novel situations reflects the same autoregressive trajectory learning that enables artificial systems to produce coherent, contextually appropriate responses. This convergence suggests that autoregressive sequence generation may represent a fundamental computational principle underlying intelligence itself, one that scales from the statistical patterns of language to the embodied dynamics of adaptive behavior, unified by the elegant simplicity of learning what comes next in the context of what came before.

Auto-Autoregression
How the Brain Learns to Write its Own Next Move
Elan Barenholtz, Ph.D.
Sep 26, 2025
