<h2 data-start="1310" data-end="1373">✅ <strong data-start="1315" data-end="1373">Paper-ready insertion (Theory or Related Work section)</strong></h2>
<h3 data-start="1375" data-end="1442"><strong data-start="1379" data-end="1442">Autoregressive Cognition and Trajectory Manifold Hypothesis</strong></h3>
<p data-start="1444" data-end="1599">The hypothesis that sequential cognition mirrors autoregressive next-token generation is developed most explicitly by Barenholtz (2025), who proposes that:</p>
<blockquote data-start="1601" data-end="2021">
<p data-start="1603" data-end="2021"><em data-start="1603" data-end="2002">“Cognition consists of a generative process in which previous states—perceptual, linguistic, motor—are used to infer the next useful cognitive state … These tokens can include motor commands, attentional shifts, fragments of inner speech, or visual imagery … all emerge from the brain’s learned ability to generate appropriate next states by navigating trajectories through cognitive token space.”</em> (Barenholtz, 2025)</p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start="2023" data-end="2328">Barenholtz further argues that cognition operates not by symbolic planning or explicit predictive modeling, but by traversing a learned <em data-start="2159" data-end="2180">trajectory manifold</em> shaped by temporal co-occurrence, Hebbian association, and reinforcement-guided path selection. In this view, long-range coherence emerges because:</p>
<blockquote data-start="2330" data-end="2495">
<p data-start="2332" data-end="2495"><em data-start="2332" data-end="2476">“The choice of a given token is thus the choice of a given path, and the rest of the sequence unfolds autoregressively along that trajectory.”</em> (Barenholtz, 2025)</p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start="2497" data-end="2668">Critically, this framework claims that next-state prediction is the <em data-start="2565" data-end="2580">action itself</em>, collapsing perception, decision, and motor planning into a single autoregressive loop:</p>
<blockquote data-start="2670" data-end="2968">
<p data-start="2672" data-end="2968"><em data-start="2672" data-end="2949">“Cognition can be understood to involve an autoregressive loop where sensory input gets processed into cognitive tokens, attention mechanisms weight recent cognitive history, and the system generates the next appropriate cognitive state based on learned trajectory patterns.”</em> (Barenholtz, 2025)</p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start="2970" data-end="3157">This model aligns with a trajectory-level solution to the credit assignment problem, where reinforcement learning does not score individual transitions but evaluates entire learned paths:</p>
<blockquote data-start="3159" data-end="3302">
<p data-start="3161" data-end="3302"><em data-start="3161" data-end="3283">“The temporal credit assignment problem dissolves because the unit of evaluation becomes the trajectory pattern itself.”</em> (Barenholtz, 2025)</p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start="3304" data-end="3553">By framing cognition as <em data-start="3328" data-end="3383">next-token prediction over internal multimodal states</em>, Barenholtz unifies language, motor control, perception, memory, and planning under a single computational principle. The present work extends this model by formalizing:</p>
<ol data-start="3555" data-end="3907">
<li data-start="3555" data-end="3661">
<p data-start="3558" data-end="3661">The information-theoretic conditions under which trajectory templates can be holographically recovered,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3662" data-end="3775">
<p data-start="3665" data-end="3775">The exact mechanism by which transformer attention approximates posterior inference over trajectory manifolds,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3776" data-end="3907">
<p data-start="3779" data-end="3907">The falsifiable empirical signatures that distinguish this framework from symbolic, modular, and predictive-coding alternatives.</p>
</li>
</ol>
