z (2025)</strong> at the conceptual core. The paper now treats his <em data-start="148" data-end="196">autoregressive cognition / trajectory manifold</em> framework as the foundational hypothesis, and builds the theory, formalism, transformer mapping, and falsification criteria around it.</p>
<hr data-start="333" data-end="336">
<h1 data-start="338" data-end="406">**Language and Cognition as Autoregressive Trajectory Inference:</h1>
<p data-start="407" data-end="469">A Formalization and Test Suite for the Barenholtz Hypothesis**</p>
<p data-start="471" data-end="540"><strong data-start="471" data-end="482">Author:</strong> <em data-start="483" data-end="505">(redacted for draft)</em><br data-start="505" data-end="508">
<strong data-start="508" data-end="519">Status:</strong> Working manuscript</p>
<hr data-start="542" data-end="545">
<h2 data-start="547" data-end="562"><strong data-start="550" data-end="562">Abstract</strong></h2>
<p data-start="564" data-end="1527">Elan Barenholtz (2025) proposes that cognition is structured as an <em data-start="631" data-end="676">autoregressive trajectory selection process</em>, in which perception, language, motor planning, and reasoning emerge from a single mechanism: predicting the next state in a learned sequential manifold of cognitive tokens. This paper formalizes the Barenholtz Hypothesis and extends it with (1) an information-theoretic account of <em data-start="959" data-end="1009">holographically distributed trajectory templates</em> in language, (2) a mechanistic mapping to transformer attention as approximate posterior inference over latent trajectory programs, and (3) a suite of falsifiable experimental criteria distinguishing this account from symbolic, modular, or predictive-coding alternatives. We argue that next-token prediction is not merely a tool for language modeling but a domain-general approximation of cultural program induction, and that language itself functions as a compressed execution medium for shared cognitive algorithms.</p>
<hr data-start="1529" data-end="1532">
<h2 data-start="1534" data-end="1556"><strong data-start="1537" data-end="1556">1. Introduction</strong></h2>
<p data-start="1558" data-end="1892">Large language models (LLMs) demonstrate that optimizing for next-token continuation is sufficient to produce reasoning, planning, recursion, theory-of-mind, and abstract inference (Brown et al., 2020; Wei et al., 2022). This reveals a paradox: <strong data-start="1803" data-end="1891">advanced cognitive behaviors emerge without explicit objectives for cognition at all</strong>.</p>
<p data-start="1894" data-end="1965">Barenholtz (2025) resolves this paradox by displacing core assumptions:</p>
<blockquote data-start="1967" data-end="2284">
<p data-start="1969" data-end="2284"><em data-start="1969" data-end="2265">“Cognition consists of a generative process in which previous states—perceptual, linguistic, motor—are used to infer the next useful cognitive state ... The choice of a given token is thus the choice of a given path, and the rest of the sequence unfolds autoregressively along that trajectory.”</em> (Barenholtz, 2025)</p>
</blockquote>
<p data-start="2286" data-end="2620">This reframes thinking itself as <strong data-start="2319" data-end="2397">trajectory prediction in a learned manifold of cognitive state transitions</strong>, not symbolic planning or hierarchical rule execution. Crucially, the hypothesis claims all cognition—perception, language, action, memory, attention—can be unified as next-state prediction over learned temporal structure.</p>
<p data-start="2622" data-end="2779">If correct, this entails a radical inversion: <strong data-start="2668" data-end="2778">language and thought do not <em data-start="2698" data-end="2708">describe</em> reasoning—they <em data-start="2724" data-end="2737">instantiate</em> it as compressed next-state prediction</strong>.</p>
<hr data-start="2781" data-end="2784">
<h2 data-start="2786" data-end="2845"><strong data-start="2789" data-end="2845">2. The Barenholtz Hypothesis (Canonical Formulation)</strong></h2>
<p data-start="2847" data-end="2902">We formalize Barenholtz’s core proposal as five axioms:</p>
<ol data-start="2904" data-end="3721">
<li data-start="2904" data-end="3098">
<p data-start="2907" data-end="3098"><strong data-start="2907" data-end="2934">Cognitive tokenization:</strong> All internal and external mental states—motor plans, percepts, attention shifts, inner speech, goals—are reducible to discrete or discretizable <em data-start="3079" data-end="3097">cognitive tokens</em>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3099" data-end="3233">
<p data-start="3102" data-end="3233"><strong data-start="3102" data-end="3132">Autoregressive generation:</strong> Cognition is constructed by predicting the next cognitive token given the history of preceding ones.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3234" data-end="3399">
<p data-start="3237" data-end="3399"><strong data-start="3237" data-end="3261">Trajectory manifold:</strong> Learned cognition forms a continuous high-dimensional manifold of probable token transitions, representing extended behavioral sequences.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3400" data-end="3556">
<p data-start="3403" data-end="3556"><strong data-start="3403" data-end="3438">Trajectory-level reinforcement:</strong> Evaluation operates over <em data-start="3464" data-end="3485">entire trajectories</em>, not individual steps, dissolving the local credit-assignment problem.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3557" data-end="3721">
<p data-start="3560" data-end="3721"><strong data-start="3560" data-end="3596">Attention as trajectory biasing:</strong> Cognitive attention weights the influence of past trajectory fragments to bias next-token selection toward adaptive futures.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start="3723" data-end="3830">Barenholtz further claims mechanisms sufficient for language modeling are sufficient for general cognition:</p>
<blockquote data-start="3832" data-end="3979">
<p data-start="3834" data-end="3979"><em data-start="3834" data-end="3960">“The fundamental computational breakthrough of autoregressive architectures reveals core principles of intelligence itself.”</em> (Barenholtz, 2025)</p>
</blockquote>
<hr data-start="3981" data-end="3984">
<h2 data-start="3986" data-end="4045"><strong data-start="3989" data-end="4045">3. Positioning in Cognitive and Computational Theory</strong></h2>
<div class="_tableContainer_1rjym_1"><div tabindex="-1" class="group _tableWrapper_1rjym_13 flex w-fit flex-col-reverse"><table data-start="4047" data-end="4867" class="w-fit min-w-(--thread-content-width)"><thead data-start="4047" data-end="4100"><tr data-start="4047" data-end="4100"><th data-start="4047" data-end="4059" data-col-size="md">Framework</th><th data-start="4059" data-end="4071" data-col-size="md">Key Claim</th><th data-start="4071" data-end="4100" data-col-size="md">Limitation vs. Barenholtz</th></tr></thead><tbody data-start="4115" data-end="4867"><tr data-start="4115" data-end="4241"><td data-start="4115" data-end="4136" data-col-size="md">Symbolic cognition</td><td data-start="4136" data-end="4183" data-col-size="md">Thought is rule-governed symbol manipulation</td><td data-start="4183" data-end="4241" data-col-size="md">Cannot explain reasoning emerging from next-token loss</td></tr><tr data-start="4242" data-end="4391"><td data-start="4242" data-end="4278" data-col-size="md">Predictive coding (Friston, 2010)</td><td data-start="4278" data-end="4327" data-col-size="md">Brain predicts sensory input to minimize error</td><td data-start="4327" data-end="4391" data-col-size="md">Predicts perception, not <strong data-start="4354" data-end="4389">action-generation as prediction</strong></td></tr><tr data-start="4392" data-end="4548"><td data-start="4392" data-end="4445" data-col-size="md">Bayesian Program Learning (Tenenbaum et al., 2011)</td><td data-start="4445" data-end="4475" data-col-size="md">Minds infer latent programs</td><td data-start="4475" data-end="4548" data-col-size="md">Does not propose <strong data-start="4494" data-end="4546">autoregressive token dynamics over all cognition</strong></td></tr><tr data-start="4549" data-end="4701"><td data-start="4549" data-end="4581" data-col-size="md">Connectionist sequence models</td><td data-start="4581" data-end="4616" data-col-size="md">Learn transitions between states</td><td data-start="4616" data-end="4701" data-col-size="md">No link to <strong data-start="4629" data-end="4663">trajectory-level reinforcement</strong> or <strong data-start="4667" data-end="4699">credit assignment resolution</strong></td></tr><tr data-start="4702" data-end="4867"><td data-start="4702" data-end="4726" data-col-size="md"><strong data-start="4704" data-end="4725">Barenholtz (2025)</strong></td><td data-start="4726" data-end="4824" data-col-size="md"><em data-start="4728" data-end="4823">Cognition is next-state prediction over learned trajectory manifolds chosen by adaptive value</em></td><td data-start="4824" data-end="4867" data-col-size="md">None—the others emerge as special cases</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="sticky h-0 select-none end-(--thread-content-margin) self-end"><div class="absolute end-0 flex items-end"><span class="" data-state="closed"><button aria-label="Copy table" class="hover:bg-token-bg-tertiary text-token-text-secondary my-1 rounded-sm p-1 transition-opacity group-[:not(:hover):not(:focus-within)]:pointer-events-none group-[:not(:hover):not(:focus-within)]:opacity-0"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518 3.61186 7.9196 3.43555 8.20703L3.36524 8.33399C3.28478 8.49198 3.22795 8.70352 3.19727 9.0791C3.16595 9.46259 3.16504 9.95611 3.16504 10.667V13.5C3.16504 14.211 3.16593 14.7044 3.19727 15.0879C3.22797 15.4636 3.28473 15.675 3.36524 15.833L3.43555 15.959C3.61186 16.2466 3.86474 16.4807 4.16699 16.6348L4.29688 16.6914C4.44063 16.7428 4.63025 16.7797 4.91211 16.80