68 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% PART II — Immanent Generation, Sparse Inference, and Manifold Geometry</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\part</span></span><span>{Immanent Generation, Sparse Inference, and Manifold Geometry}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Language as a Predictive Geometric Object}

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the space of latent world-trajectories and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> the token vocabulary.
A language community generates sequences by sampling
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}}(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:T}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span>), </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> p(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
inducing a marginal predictive model
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
p(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t}) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span> p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}}(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span>,x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t})</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t})</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}

A learner forms a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{sparse posterior} over latent continuations:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i=1}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> k </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ll</span></span><span> |</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span>|,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
giving efficient next-step inference:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
p(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i=1}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}}(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}

This is mathematically equivalent to top-</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> hypothesis tracking in Rao–Blackwellized filtering and to sparse belief-maintenance in Active Inference </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{friston2010,hohwy2013}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Predictive Manifold and Fisher–Rao Metric}

Define parameters </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> as the local predictive logits of a model conditioned on context </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. The induced Riemannian metric on the manifold of predictive contexts is the Fisher–Rao metric:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{ab}(x) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{y </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>[ </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\log</span></span><span> p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(y</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x)}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\log</span></span><span> p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(y</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x)}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>].
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}

Semantic similarity corresponds to geodesic proximity:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
d(x,y) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\inf</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sqrt</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\top</span></span><span> g(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>(s)) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>}}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>ds.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}

Curvature proxies computable from real models include:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{align}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{Trace Fisher: } </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\operatorname</span></span><span>{tr}(g) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{aa}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{Entropy gradient: } </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x H(p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x)) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{Discrete curvature: } </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span>(x,x',x'') =
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{KL}(p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{x'} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{KL}(p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{x''}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{x'}) - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{KL}(p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{x''}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{align}

High curvature implies strong sparsification: only few </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> remain viable over short context displacements.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Immanent Template Extraction Without Symbols}

We adopt Barenholtz’s key claim:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{quote}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{``The learning system does not merely predict the next token—it learns to predict the apparatus that predicts the next token. The model is not autoregressive, it is auto-autoregressive.''} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{barenholtz2025}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{quote}

Formally, if ordinary autoregression fits:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span> = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\arg</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\min</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}[-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\log</span></span><span> p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t})],
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
auto-autoregression implies a higher-order objective:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span> = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\arg</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\min</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Theta</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>[ -</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\log</span></span><span> p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Theta</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>],
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denotes the local predictive operator itself. The ``template'' is thus not stored symbolically but exists as a fixed point of predictor–predictor dynamics.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% PART III — Developmental and Evolutionary Alignment</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\part</span></span><span>{Developmental and Evolutionary Alignment}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Child Language Bootstrapping as Sparse Predictive Optimization}

Infants do not enumerate grammars </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{chomsky1957}, but compress predictive uncertainty under perceptual constraints. Let a child maintain a hypothesis set </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dots</span></span><span>,h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> of generative micro-constructions (not symbolic grammars). Update follows Bayesian sparsification:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
p(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\propto</span></span><span> p(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\prod</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{j</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t} p(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
followed by *pruning*:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leftarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> p(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t}) &gt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}

This reproduces empirical developmental phenomena without invoking discrete symbolic induction: overgeneralization, U-shaped learning, and rapid syntactic stabilization arise from compression dynamics, not rule enumeration </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{kirby2001,tamariz2015}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Phylogenetic Compression Across Populations}

Iterated cultural transmission applies repeated sparse posterior truncation across generations:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}(h) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\propto</span></span><span> [p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t(h)]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>[L(h)]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\beta</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
amplifying compressible hypotheses and eliminating high-description-length ones, matching observed trends toward compositionality and structural reuse </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{tamariz2015}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% PART IV — Transformer Dynamics as Predictive Geometry</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\part</span></span><span>{Transformer Dynamics as Predictive Geometry}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Attention as Posterior Routing}

Attention implements sparse Bayesian model averaging:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{tj} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\propto</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exp</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{q</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\top</span></span><span> k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> p(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span>=j </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t}),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
with top-</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> heads functioning as mixture components over latent trajectories.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Layers as Unrolled Natural-Gradient Inference}

Model updates approximate natural gradient descent on negative log likelihood:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1} = h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span> g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
consistent with transformer layers behaving as discretized geodesic integrators on the predictive manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{aten2021}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Heads as Basis Hypotheses}

Each attention head </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> implements a low-rank hypothesis subspace:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
p(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t}),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}
where pruning inactive heads approximates hypothesis sparsification.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% PART V — Moderate Nativist Reconciliation</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\part</span></span><span>{Moderate Reconciliation with Nativist Constraints}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Where Bias Is, and Is Not}

The model accepts:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Strong inductive biases for efficiency (hierarchical prediction, sparsity, locality, compositional reuse).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Innate constraints on perception–action cycles, working memory bottlenecks, and entropy minimization pressure.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

It rejects:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Encoded symbolic grammars or algebraic syntax trees as primitive.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Language-specific innate operators.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

Thus, inductive bias exists, but exists at the level of predictive dynamics, not discrete symbolic formalisms.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% PART VI — Falsification and Empirical Regime</span></span><span>
