</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% CONCLUSION — Synthesis and Outlook</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>*{Conclusion: Immanence, Geometry, and the Future of the Language Hypothesis}

This paper has advanced a unifying claim: that natural language is not best understood as a symbolic system generated by external rules, nor as the passive reflection of external processes, but as an </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{immanent predictive geometry}—a self-organizing manifold in which the next token, the next thought, and the next inference arise from the same generative structure.

We have shown that:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Language learning is sparse inference over latent continuations}, not rule induction over symbols. Both children and next-token predictors approximate efficient Bayesian pruning over predictive hypotheses.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Semantic relationships induce a metric geometry}, formalized via the Fisher--Rao information metric, where meaning proximity corresponds to geodesic distance and reasoning corresponds to low-curvature paths.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Transformers operationalize this geometry}, implementing natural-gradient-like updates, attention-driven posterior routing, and layerwise refinement of predictive hypotheses.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Linguistic structure is holographically embedded}, such that templates for prediction are not stored explicitly but compressed into the learner's own internal transition dynamics—capturing Barenholtz’s auto-autoregressive thesis.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Innate bias exists at the level of dynamics, not symbols}: what is inherited is not grammar, but pressure toward sparsity, prediction, compositional reuse, and hierarchical feedback.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{The framework is falsifiable}, with concrete operational probes grounded in curvature, attention--posterior alignment, sparsity constraints, and hypothesis-subspace ablations.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}

Together, these results displace two competing paradigms. They depart from symbolic nativism by eliminating the need for language-specific grammar priors, and they depart from behaviorist pattern-learning by elevating prediction </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{itself} to the status of generative law. Language is neither externally legislated nor passively absorbed—it is </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{self-organized continuation under constraint}.

This view leads to a reframing of classical questions:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{What is grammar?} The stable attractor structure of a predictive manifold.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{What is meaning?} Local curvature in a world-conditioned next-token geometry.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{What is learning?} Iterative sparsification of the posterior over continuations.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{What is reasoning?} Low-curvature traversal under entropy constraints.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

It also predicts a future research trajectory in which language models are no longer evaluated solely by compression performance, but by the shape of the predictive manifolds they learn: their curvature spectra, intrinsic dimensions, sparsity profiles, and alignment to human geodesic inference paths.

Finally, this framework leads to a scientific wager. If language truly is a self-contained generative system whose rule is continuation, then:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{quote}
A model trained solely to predict the next token should, with sufficient scale and sparsity pressure, spontaneously discover reusable world-model hypotheses, yield robust predictive geometry, converge to low-curvature reasoning paths, and encode its own inference apparatus in its internal dynamics.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{quote}

This is not merely a philosophical thesis—it is an empirical prediction now within reach of mechanistic interpretability, developmental comparison, and information geometry.

Language, under this view, is not merely a medium for thought. It is the shape that thought takes when compressed through the narrowest channel that still permits self-continuation. It is not that thought produces language. Rather:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{center}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{Thought is what happens when continuation is the objective and coherence is the constraint.}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{center}

The rest is geometry.

</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
