\title{Language as a Holographic Predictive Medium in Autoregressive Cognition: Distributed Trajectory Templates, Sparse Inference, and Emergent Computation in Next-Token Learners}
\author{(Redacted for Draft)}
\date{}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The emergent capabilities of large language models demonstrate that optimizing for next-token prediction over natural language corpora is sufficient to recover reasoning, planning, recursion, and algorithmic generalization without explicit symbolic architectures or external reward. Building upon the Barenholtz Hypothesis (Barenholtz, 2025), which posits cognition as autoregressive trajectory selection within a learned manifold of cognitive tokens, we advance the view that natural language functions as a holographically encoded predictive medium—a distributed substrate embedding reusable trajectory templates retrievable via sparse inference. Comprehension constitutes model selection over latent computational programs, semantics form a navigable geometry induced by contextual transitions, and transformer attention approximates posterior inference over candidate generative trajectories. We formalize this integrated hypothesis, ground it in predictive processing, Bayesian program induction, information geometry, and autoregressive cognition, map mechanistic claims to transformers, and derive testable predictions with rigorous falsification criteria. Language emerges not merely as a communicative vehicle but as a lossy, shared execution surface for cultural trajectory algorithms.
\end{abstract}

