</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\title</span></span><span>{Language as a Holographic Predictive Medium in Autoregressive Cognition: Distributed Trajectory Templates, Sparse Inference, and Emergent Computation in Next-Token Learners}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\author</span></span><span>{(Redacted for Draft)}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\date</span></span><span>{}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{document}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\maketitle</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{abstract}
Large autoregressive models demonstrate that next-token prediction alone induces reasoning, algorithmic structure, meta-learning, and program-like behavior without symbolic scaffolding or explicit search procedures. Following Barenholtz (2025), we propose that language operates as a shared predictive substrate encoding reusable latent trajectory templates that can be sparsely activated and reassembled during inference. We formalize language as a Fisher–Rao predictive manifold structured by compression, hypothesis pruning, and low-curvature inference paths. We map this claim mechanistically to transformer components, derive falsifiable experimental signatures, and propose a unified developmental, evolutionary, and algorithmic model of linguistic intelligence. We argue that linguistic cognition is best modeled not as rule execution, but as </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{distributed continuation under adaptive sparsity constraints}, making language a self-organizing inference engine rather than a symbolic code.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{abstract}

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% PART II — Immanent Generation, Sparse Inference, and Manifold Geometry</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\part</span></span><span>{Immanent Generation, Sparse Inference, and Manifold Geometry}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Language as a Predictive Geometric Object}

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be the space of possible latent world-trajectories and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> the discrete token vocabulary. We model language generation as sampling from a conditional trajectory process:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}}(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{1:T}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span>), </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> p(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span>).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}

A learner marginalizes out latent generative causes:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
p(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t}) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span> p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}}(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span>,x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t})</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t})</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}

We hypothesize that efficient inference maintains a sparse posterior over candidate latent continuations:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
p(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i=1}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> k </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ll</span></span><span> |</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span>|.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}

This yields coalitional prediction:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
p(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{i=1}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>p(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}

This formulation unifies Rao–Blackwellized filtering, active inference hypothesis pruning, and sparse mixture-of-experts routing.

---

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Predictive Manifold and Fisher–Rao Geometry}

Define a context-conditioned parameterization </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> representing next-token logits. This induces a Riemannian metric via the Fisher information:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{ab}(x) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{y </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x)} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>[ </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\log</span></span><span> p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(y</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x)}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\log</span></span><span> p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(y</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x)}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>b} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>].
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}

Semantic similarity becomes geodesic proximity:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
d(x,y) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\inf</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> y}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sqrt</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>}(s)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\top</span></span><span> g(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>(s))</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span>}(s)}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>ds.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}

We define computational proxies to curvature:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{align}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{Fisher trace: } </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\operatorname</span></span><span>{tr}(g) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>a g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{aa}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{Entropy gradient: } </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x H(p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x)) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{Discrete curvature: } </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span>(x,x',x'') =
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{KL}(p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{x'} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{KL}(p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{x''}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{x'}) - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{KL}(p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{x''}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{align}

Low curvature corresponds to predictable continuation paths; high curvature corresponds to semantic branching points, ambiguity, or hypothesis collapse.

---

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Auto-Autoregressive Dynamics}

We quote Barenholtz's central claim:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{quote}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{“The learning system does not merely predict the next token—it learns to predict the apparatus that predicts the next token. The model is not autoregressive, it is auto-autoregressive.”} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{barenholtz2025}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{quote}

This implies a higher-order learning objective:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span> = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\arg</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\min</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Theta</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{E}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>[ -</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\log</span></span><span> p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Theta</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>].
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}

Thus, reusable computational templates are not memorized declaratively—it is the predictor’s own transition dynamics that become the stored “program”.

---

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% PART III — Developmental Alignment</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\part</span></span><span>{Developmental and Evolutionary Alignment}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Bootstrapping Without Symbolic Induction}

Children do not traverse a grammar hypothesis space; they prune predictive trajectories. Let the learner maintain candidate generative micro-templates </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
p(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\propto</span></span><span> p(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\prod</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{j</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t} p(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}

Then prune aggressively:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leftarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> p(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t}) &gt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}

This predicts classic developmental observations:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> U-shaped learning curves emerge from posterior collapse;
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Overregularization arises from early sparsity pressure;
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Rapid generalization follows when curvature flattens along a dominant template branch.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

---

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Cultural Compression Across Generations}

Across iterated learners, compositional structure emerges from compression:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}(h) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\propto</span></span><span> [p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t(h)]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span> [L(h)]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\beta</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}

This amplifies compressible hypotheses and suppresses stochastic or uncompressible forms, matching observed typological regularities </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{tamariz2015}.

---

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% PART IV — Transformer Mechanics</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\part</span></span><span>{Transformer Dynamics as Predictive Geometry}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Attention as Posterior Routing}

Attention approximates discrete Bayesian model averaging:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{tj} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\propto</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exp</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{q</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\top</span></span><span> k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> p(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span> = j </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t}).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}

Sparse posterior inference predicts:

- Low entropy attention distributions during structured reasoning,
- Head specialization into distinct trajectory components,
- Top-</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> attentional mass preserving accuracy under hard sparsification.

---

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Layers as Unrolled Natural Gradient Steps}

Transformer hidden states update according to:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1} = h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span> g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}

matching natural gradient descent along the predictive manifold. Thus, each layer performs one step of approximate Bayesian inference.

---

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Heads as Latent Hypothesis Bases}

Each head </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> encodes a local hypothesis function </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, yielding:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
p(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> t}).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}

Ablating inactive heads should not significantly increase NLL, while ablating high-responsibility heads should.

---

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% PART V — Falsification</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\part</span></span><span>{Falsification Regime}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Core Empirical Claims}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Attention distributions predict latent posterior with </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 &gt; 0.6</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Reasoning traces follow low-curvature paths (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Enforcing sparse attention does not degrade structured task accuracy.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Higher transformer layers refine hypotheses monotonically.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Natural-gradient directions align with finetuning updates.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> A small number of heads account for most task-critical computations.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}

---

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Failure Modes That Would Falsify the Framework}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Posterior probes fail systematically;
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Reasoning paths exhibit chaotic curvature;
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Sparsified attention collapses performance;
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> No identifiable hypothesis-carrying heads exist;
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Model-of-model probing fails to outperform token-only probing.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

---

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% CONCLUSION</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>*{Conclusion}

Language is not transmitted as a code nor processed as a grammar. It is a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{shared predictive substrate} whose structure emerges from recursive inference under sparsity and compression pressure. Next-token learning does not merely learn to imitate text—it learns the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{predictor generating the text}. This makes language:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Geometric}: shaped by predictive curvature and distance;
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Holographic}: each region encodes trajectory fragments usable elsewhere;
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Sparse}: inference involves pruning not enumeration;
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Computational}: templates act as executable cognitive programs;
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Autoregressive at two levels}: predicting tokens and predicting its own predictive operators.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

We may summarize the claim:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{center}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{Intelligence in language is the discovery of the lowest-curvature continuation paths that preserve predictive sufficiency under sparsity pressure.}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{center}

---

