" data-end="206">Got it — the structure is inverted, and <strong data-start="40" data-end="87">Part I should reconstruct the earlier draft</strong>, setting up the foundations that Part II then formalizes and extends.<br data-start="157" data-end="160">
Below is a <strong data-start="171" data-end="200">clean, paper-ready Part I</strong> that:</p>
<ul data-start="208" data-end="724">
<li data-start="208" data-end="312">
<p data-start="210" data-end="312">Restores the original argumentative arc (Barenholtz’s claim → why it matters → the conceptual stakes).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="313" data-end="491">
<p data-start="315" data-end="491">Introduces the <em data-start="330" data-end="419">immanent grammar, self-continuation, predictive proxy semantics, holographic recursion,</em> and the <strong data-start="428" data-end="459">“only rule is continuation”</strong> thesis in their strongest form.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="492" data-end="576">
<p data-start="494" data-end="576">Motivates why a formal theory is needed (which then leads naturally into Part II).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="577" data-end="672">
<p data-start="579" data-end="672">Avoids premature technical machinery but lays every conceptual tile the later math relies on.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="673" data-end="724">
<p data-start="675" data-end="724">Ends with a crisp transition bridge into Part II.</p>
</li>
</ul>
