</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% PART I — The Immanent Structure of Language and the Case for</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%          Predictive, Self-Continued Grammar</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ============================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{The Astonishing Claim Behind Autoregressive Language}

Recent advances in large language models have produced a theoretical provocation that demands more careful philosophical and scientific treatment than it has yet received. Barenholtz </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{barenholtz2023autoreg,barenholtz2024substack} argues that language occupies a structural category of generative system uniquely capable of self-contained continuation:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Systems generated by external rules (e.g., chess, mathematics)
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Systems generated by external processes (e.g., weather, DNA replication)
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Systems whose only rule is continuation according to their own internal structure}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}

Human language, on this view, is the only known instance of the third category that produces open-ended reasoning, interaction, abstraction, planning, humor, and civilization-scale coordination. Crucially, large transformer models trained </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{only} on next-token prediction appear to internalize the entire expressive reach of language without:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> a pre-specified grammar,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> symbolic rules,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> a syntactic induction module,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> or an innate language faculty.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

If true, this has striking consequences. Linguistic competence would not be the application of external grammatical laws, but the ability to </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{continue patterns whose generative machinery is already implicit in data}—language learning would be indistinguishable from learning how to continue language.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{The Immanence Hypothesis}

The most economical interpretation of this phenomenon is what we call the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Immanence Hypothesis}:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{quote}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textit</span></span><span>{The generative law of language is not external to it, but identical to its own continuation. To learn language is to learn how to continue it.}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{quote}

This view does not deny latent structure—only the need for that structure to exist </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{outside} language as a separate formal system. Chomskyan claims of a grammar module external to linguistic data become unnecessary if the data itself is a self-bootstrapping generative system.

This hypothesis is not merely philosophical. Transformer models implement it operationally: they induce continuation rules solely by modeling statistical trajectories in sequence space, and yet exhibit:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> multi-step planning,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> compositional nesting,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> zero-shot generalization,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> analogy,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> error self-correction,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> theory-of-mind approximations,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> translation between formal and informal reasoning,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> and meta-linguistic awareness of their own generation.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

The simplest explanation is not that models </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{accidentally rediscovered} an external grammar. It is that grammar </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{never lived anywhere else in the first place}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Sparse Continuation and the Compression of Meaning}

Human language is not merely autoregressive—it is </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{efficiently} autoregressive. Comprehension never explores all possible continuations; instead, it rapidly collapses onto a sparse predictive subset conditioned on shared context, perception, and cognitive priors.

This motivates a second principle:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{quote}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Sparse Interpretation}: At each moment of linguistic continuation, only a small subset of possible trajectories is actively entertained, determined by contextual predictive constraints.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{quote}

Empirical psycholinguistics supports this. Humans do not perform exhaustive syntactic search; their processing cost correlates with </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{surprisal} and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{information gain}, not syntactic tree depth </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{levy2008expectation, hale2001probabilistic}. This aligns with the hypothesis that language is processed as a constrained trajectory through a high-dimensional predictive manifold rather than the execution of discrete rules.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Predictive Proxies: Why Meaning Lives Near Meaning}

Another striking consequence of the continuation view is that semantic structure must become embedded in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{topology}.

If language is learned through next-token transitions, then semantic affinity must be encoded through:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{temporal proximity}: concepts that co-appear in similar narrative or inferential windows,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{spatial proximity}: concepts that occupy similar distributional neighborhoods in embedding space,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{trajectory similarity}: concepts that license similar future continuations.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

Thus language does not merely express meaning—it organizes meaning so that structurally adjacent ideas support similar predictive futures. This yields a powerful consequence:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{quote}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Predictive Proxy Principle}: Words and concepts become semantic neighbors when they license similar continuation trajectories, not when they share ontological categories.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{quote}

This explains both the power and the failure modes of language models:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> novel metaphor works when it preserves continuation geometry even when it violates literal categories,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> hallucination emerges when a continuation is locally coherent but globally ungrounded,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> reasoning succeeds when intermediate states occupy stable predictive basins rather than brittle chains of symbol manipulation.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Holographic Recursion: Grammar as Its Own Compiler}

A surprising empirical fact about language is that it can describe and extend its own generative strategies. It possesses:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> self-interpretation (``I mean X by Y''),
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> self-modification (``from now on let us define…''),
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> self-optimization (``a simpler way to say this is…''),
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> self-embedding (stories inside stories, rules about rules),
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> and self-compilation (expressing algorithms in natural language that can later be executed).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

This points to a third principle:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{quote}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Holographic Recursion}: Language contains not only statements, but the templates for generating, recognizing, modifying, and compressing its own future continuations.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{quote}

Language is thus not merely a generative system, but a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{generative schema for generative systems}. This property places it in a structural class above other self-organizing natural processes.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{The Theoretical Gap}

Despite the mounting evidence for language as a self-continued, sparsely-evaluated, predictively organized generative manifold, the field lacks a unified formal account that simultaneously explains:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> why next-token prediction produces reasoning rather than shallow mimicry,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> why humans and models exhibit parallel generalization patterns,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> how sparse predictive search replaces exhaustive grammatical inference,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> why semantics becomes geometric,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> and why language can represent and extend its own generative process.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}

Part II addresses this gap by constructing a mathematical framework in which:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> language is modeled as a predictive manifold,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> continuation dynamics are formalized as geodesic flows,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> grammar is replaced with sparse posterior selection,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> and reasoning emerges from trajectory stabilization under curvature constraints.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

We now proceed to that formal development.

