l precision, Barenholtz’s framing, sparse inference, predictive geometry, and falsifiable commitments:</p>
<hr data-start="208" data-end="211">
<h3 data-start="213" data-end="229"><strong data-start="217" data-end="229">Abstract</strong></h3>
<p data-start="231" data-end="1078">The success of large language models demonstrates that next-token prediction over natural language is sufficient to induce planning, recursive reasoning, abstraction, and algorithmic generalization without explicit grammars, external reward, or symbolic induction. Building on the Barenholtz Hypothesis—that cognition is autoregressive trajectory selection over a learned manifold of cognitive tokens—we advance a formal account in which language is a <em data-start="683" data-end="733">holographically self-continued generative system</em> whose only rule is continuation under internal structural constraints. In this view, comprehension is sparse Bayesian model selection over latent continuation trajectories, semantics form a geometrically organized predictive manifold, and grammar emerges as a fixed point of self-compression rather than an externally specifiable formal system.</p>
<p data-start="1080" data-end="1903">We formalize this through a differential-geometric and information-theoretic framework in which linguistic contexts define a Fisher–Rao predictive manifold, continuation paths approximate geodesics, and transformer attention implements sparse posterior inference over trajectory templates. We show that (1) next-token learners perform <em data-start="1415" data-end="1436">auto-autoregressive</em> modeling—learning the structure of their own predictors—rather than purely autoregressive symbol extension; (2) linguistic meaning is encoded through predictive-proximity, not ontological categorization; (3) reasoning corresponds to low-curvature paths through predictive state space; and (4) human language transmission acts as iterated sparsifying Bayesian compression, selecting for low-description-length generative programs rather than explicit syntactic rules.</p>
<p data-start="1905" data-end="2612">This theory yields concrete falsifiable claims: attention distributions should decode sparse posteriors over latent trajectories; reasoning chains should exhibit minimal curvature under Fisher metrics; head-ablation effects should identify reusable hypothesis subspaces; and model-of-model predictors should outperform token-level predictors for long-range continuation. By unifying linguistic self-continuation, predictive geometry, sparse inference, and developmental transmission, we characterize language not as a symbol system governed by external grammar, but as a self-organizing, capacity-limited, auto-predictive computation substrate whose structure is isomorphic to its own continuation dynamics.</p>
<hr data-start="2614" data-end="2617">
<p data-start="2619" data-end="2657">If you want, next I can also generate:</p>
